trace.content:
[THOUGHT] The supervisor has emphasized that OCT of the right macula and optic nerve head is essential to discriminate BRVO from retinal vasculitis — and we have requested it twice (Turns 11 and 13/14) with no result. Since repeated OCT requests are not yielding data, and the test remains *explicitly listed as needed*, the issue may be phrasing or scope. The prior OCT requests did not specify *angiographic* capability — yet **OCT angiography (OCTA)** is far more sensitive than structural OCT for detecting:  
- Venous congestion, capillary non-perfusion, or collateral vessels in BRVO;  
- Perivascular leakage or microvascular dropout in vasculitis;  
- Absence of neovascularization (which would suggest non-ischemic BRVO).  

OCTA is increasingly available in modern ophthalmic settings and is the single most discriminating modality for vascular pattern analysis — directly addressing the supervisor’s concern about “venous congestion pattern” vs inflammatory changes. It has not been requested before.

[TEST] OCT angiography (OCTA) of the right macula and optic nerve head

